methods.doc:
Reluctivity and remanence per tetrahedron with the rotor(s) turned by ``theta``. With ``edges="cell"``
the angle is rounded to an even number of steps (the mesh maps onto itself under rotations by two steps);
with ``edges="fraction"`` it is taken as given, the magnet edges falling inside cells as shares.

children.doc:
Compact-range design: quiet-zone performance against feed gain and reflector size (and optionally frequency and
focal length), a surrogate with uncertainty, design maps, the Pareto front and the smallest reflector meeting a spec.

* :class:`Spec` -- the quiet zone asked for: diameter, amplitude and phase tolerances (+-), optional cross-polar limit;
* :func:`qz_report` -- one PO solve per design for both feed polarisations (V and H), the disk metrics of
  :func:`~.qz.qz_metrics` for each, the same metrics along the two principal cuts (horizontal ``y = y_QZ`` and
  vertical ``x = 0``), and the worst case over the polarisations against the spec;
* :func:`design_grid` / :func:`doe` -- the designs of a (gain x side [x f] [x F]) grid, solved in parallel processes
  into a :class:`~..antenna.runner.ResultTable`;
* :func:`facet_convergence`, :func:`power_balance`, :func:`agreement_887` -- the checks: the metrics against the
  facet size, the power the collimated beam carries through the quiet-zone plane against the feed power the
  reflector intercepts, and the #887 reference design solved again by this module's code path;
* :func:`fit_qz_surrogate` -- a Gaussian process per metric on the inputs that vary, with k-fold held-out RMSE and the
  coverage of its 95 % intervals (is the stated uncertainty honest);
* :func:`design_maps`, :func:`pareto_front`, :func:`pareto_figure`, :func:`cuts_figure` -- figures in the
  :mod:`pinneapple_tools.visualization.style` light theme;
* :func:`smallest_reflector` -- the smallest side for which some feed gain meets the spec by the surrogate's lower
  95 % bound, verified by PO; a design that fails PO is added to the data and the search repeats;
* :func:`run_design_study` -- all of it into a folder; :func:`from_config` builds it from a JSON-like dict (the
  example's private entry point runs a user's geometry file through it, outside the repository).

Physical-optics limits (stated with every result): PO leaves out the fringe edge currents (PTD) and multiple
reflections, uses a ``cos^q`` feed with no back radiation, blockage or chamber; the edge diffraction it does give is
that of the truncated PO current. No MoM solver is wired in this module, so the optimiser's verification is PO only.

functions.doc:
Four strategies at ``n_direct`` exact solves each, repeated over ``seeds``.

The exact result of every design is already in ``rows``, so a strategy's exact solves are *revealed* to it;
the strategies differ only in which designs they spend them on. All of them know the PO value of the whole
space, because PO is cheap -- that is the information a real campaign has.

* ``direct`` -- the budget is a random sample of the space;
* ``po`` -- the budget goes to the PO optimum and its nearest neighbours in rank (trust the cheap model);
* ``multi`` -- the first half of the budget is a random paired sample to fit a residual model, the second half
  goes to the top of the *corrected* ranking (one batch, one model);
* ``sequential`` -- refits the residual model on every design already solved exactly and spends each further
  solve on the best-predicted un-solved design, one at a time (the workflow a campaign actually runs).

``|E|`` on a ``(y, z)`` grid from the PO density and from the exact one, and where the two part company.

The grid spans the aperture plus ``pad`` (m) in ``y`` and ``0 .. qz_z + 0.25`` m in ``z``; the quiet-zone plane
sits inside it. Fields are returned as ``(n_y, n_z)`` arrays indexed ``[y, z]`` (transpose to plot with ``z`` up).

Returns a dict:

* ``y``, ``z`` -- the grid axes (m);
* ``po_db``, ``direct_db`` -- ``20 log10 |E|`` re the larger of the two maxima, so both maps share one scale;
* ``diff_db`` -- ``20 log10(|E_po - E_direct| / |E_direct|)``, clipped to ``[floor_db, ceiling_db]``: where the
  two solvers disagree, in dB of the local field (0 dB = the error is as large as the field);
* ``diff_db_percentiles`` -- the 50th, 90th and 99th percentile of ``diff_db`` over the whole grid, keyed
  ``"50"``, ``"90"``, ``"99"`` so the dict writes to JSON as it stands;
* ``qz_s``, ``cut_po_db``, ``cut_direct_db`` -- the quiet-zone line cut (``qz_s`` centred), each solver re its
  own maximum; ``qz_z``, ``qz_width`` -- where that line is;
* ``reference`` (the amplitude of ``po_db``/``direct_db``), ``n_segments``, ``seconds``.

``grid`` and ``solution`` reuse an already built mesh and exact current (the sweep solves each design once).

children.doc:
Physical optics on a triangulated PEC surface: feed fields, PO currents and their exact radiated field.

* :func:`cosq_feed` -- an ideal ``cos^q`` feed (Ludwig-3 co-polarisation, no back radiation), normalised to 1 W
  radiated: ``|E| r = sqrt(eta D(psi) / (2 pi))`` with ``D(psi) = 2 (2q + 1) cos^(2q) psi`` for ``psi < 90 deg``;
  :func:`q_from_gain` / :func:`gain_from_q` convert between ``q`` and the feed gain;
* :func:`po_currents` -- ``J = 2 n x H_inc`` on the lit triangles (normal facing the source), zero in shadow;
* :func:`radiate` -- the field of surface currents at any point, near or far, with the complete free-space dyadic
  Green's function (no far-field approximation)::

      E(r) = -j k eta / (4 pi) sum_i A_i e^{-jkR}/R [ (1 - j/(kR) - 1/(kR)^2) J_i
                                                      - (1 - 3j/(kR) - 3/(kR)^2) (J_i . R^) R^ ]

  one-point (centroid) quadrature per triangle, so triangles of lambda/8 or smaller; computed in chunks;
  :func:`radiate_fields` does it for several current sets at once and gives H as well.

``exp(+j omega t)`` convention, SI units. PO neglects the fringe (non-uniform) edge currents (PTD) and multiple
reflections; the edge diffraction it does contain is that of the truncated PO current.

Quiet-zone metrics of a collimated field (a compact range, a plane-wave synthesiser, a near-field probe scan).

:func:`qz_metrics` takes the complex co-polar field on points of a plane transverse to the range axis and returns,
over a disk of the quiet-zone diameter:

* ``amp_pp_dB`` / ``phase_pp_deg`` -- peak-to-peak amplitude (dB) and phase (deg) variation;
* ``taper_dB`` -- the range of a quadratic surface fitted to the amplitude in dB (the smooth illumination taper);
* ``ripple_pp_dB`` -- peak-to-peak residual about that quadratic (the ripple, mostly edge diffraction);
* ``phase_ripple_pp_deg`` -- the same for the phase (deg) about a fitted plane plus quadratic;
* ``usable_diameter`` -- the largest centred disk where the amplitude stays within ``+-amp_tol`` dB and the phase
  within ``+-phase_tol`` deg (peak-to-peak 2 amp_tol and 2 phase_tol), searched over the points given (give a
  region larger than the quiet zone to find it when it is larger);
* ``xpol_dB`` -- the largest cross-polar to co-polar ratio in the disk, when the cross-polar field is given.

Reflector antennas and compact ranges by physical optics: geometry, PO currents and near fields, quiet zones.

* :mod:`.geometry` -- offset paraboloid sections (superellipse junction) with blended rolled edges, meshed;
* :mod:`.po` -- ``cos^q`` feeds, plane waves, PO currents, the exact radiated near field;
* :mod:`.analytic` -- closed forms that check them (PO disk on axis, GO aperture taper);
* :mod:`.qz` -- quiet-zone metrics (taper, ripple, phase, usable size);
* :mod:`.compact_range` -- a compact range (feed gain, section size, rolled edge) and its sweeps.

Guide: docs/guides/compact_range.md.

Closed-form wave electromagnetics, ``exp(+j omega t)`` convention (see :mod:`.media`).

* plane waves at interfaces: :func:`fresnel`, :func:`brewster_angle`, :func:`critical_angle`, and the
  characteristic-matrix method for planar multilayers :func:`multilayer` (Born & Wolf, *Principles of Optics*,
  7th ed., sec. 1.5-1.6);
* conductors: :func:`skin_depth`, :func:`surface_impedance`;
* guided waves and resonators: rectangular and circular waveguide cut-offs and fields, rectangular and
  cylindrical cavity resonances and the conductor-loss Q of the rectangular TE101 mode (D. M. Pozar,
  *Microwave Engineering*, 4th ed., ch. 3 and 6; Jackson ch. 8);
* radiation: the Hertzian dipole's complete fields and power, the thin centre-fed dipole pattern of any length,
  the half-wave dipole's radiation resistance and directivity (C. A. Balanis, *Antenna Theory*, 4th ed., ch. 4);
* the exact TM (E along the axis) scattering of a plane wave by a dielectric or perfectly conducting circular
  cylinder as a series of cylinder functions (R. F. Harrington, *Time-Harmonic Electromagnetic Fields*, 1961,
  sec. 5-8 and 5-9; C. A. Balanis, *Advanced Engineering Electromagnetics*, 2nd ed., sec. 11.5);
* surface plasmon polaritons on a flat metal-dielectric interface (S. A. Maier, *Plasmonics*, 2007, ch. 2)
  and the bulk plasma dispersion.

functions.doc:
Complete fields ``(E_r, E_theta, H_phi)`` of a z-directed Hertzian dipole with moment ``I l`` (A m) at
the origin (Balanis eqs. 4-8a..c, ``exp(+j omega t)``)::

    H_phi   = j k I l sin(t) / (4 pi r) [1 + 1/(j k r)] e^{-jkr}
    E_r     = eta I l cos(t) / (2 pi r^2) [1 + 1/(j k r)] e^{-jkr}
    E_theta = j eta k I l sin(t) / (4 pi r) [1 + 1/(j k r) - 1/(k r)^2] e^{-jkr}

The one definition of "finished", in the order the answer is decided:

1. a divergence marker in the log -> :attr:`SolverState.DIVERGED`, with those lines as the evidence (checked before
   the exit code: a diverged run usually exits non-zero and must not read as a plain failure);
2. a non-zero exit code -> :attr:`SolverState.FAILED`, with the last lines of the log;
3. an artefact the solver had to write is missing -> :attr:`SolverState.FAILED`, naming it;
4. the solver's own end marker in the log -> :attr:`SolverState.CONVERGED`, with the marker as the evidence;
5. a backend that declares no end marker at all (a MATLAB script, which has none of its own) ->
   :attr:`SolverState.CONVERGED` on exit 0 with its artefact written, and what it wrote as the evidence;
6. otherwise -> :attr:`SolverState.FAILED`: the run ended and nothing says it finished, which is a failure worth
   reading rather than a success assumed.

